The largest surface on this site is its geography: 100 city pages and 11
corridor pages out of 261 indexed URLs. Not one of them contained a form.

Their primary button, twice per city page, was:

    <a href="/#quote" className="btn btn-copper btn-lg">

That is a CROSS-PAGE anchor. A homeowner who searched "hardwood flooring
etobicoke", landed on the Etobicoke page, and pressed the main call to
action was sent to the homepage to scroll for a form. The only contact
mechanism on the page itself was a bare tel: link in the sixth section. The
corridor pages had no call to action at all.

THIS IS A REGRESSION WITH A COMMIT MESSAGE ALREADY WRITTEN ABOUT IT

EstimateForm's own docblock records F-160: "Every commercial page's primary
button pointed at /#quote. That anchor is on a DIFFERENT url... <form> count
0, <input> count 0." That fix reached /services, /pricing, /commercial,
/realtors and the four Toronto landing pages. It never reached the hundred
and eleven pages that carry the entire geographic footprint — six service
pages got a form, 111 local-intent pages did not.

A defect a previous commit message already describes is one a test should be
holding. geo-conversion.test.ts is now holding it.

WHAT MEAS-01 AND MEAS-02 BOUGHT

`source` carries the city or the corridor, so a lead from Etobicoke is
distinguishable from a lead from Oakville in the funnel ledger. "Which
cities actually produce deposits" stops being an assumption — and the answer
is what should decide where the next real job photograph comes from, because
content/job-cards.ts holds five real jobs, all in Toronto proper, so 96 of
these pages currently carry true prose about the place and no evidence at
all that this business has worked there. That gap cannot be closed by
writing; it is closed by photographing real work, and the ledger is what
says which city to photograph first.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO

It does not prefill a location. `source` says which PAGE produced the lead;
it is never a claim about where the visitor is, and it is not written into
the address field. Somebody reading the Whitby page from a Toronto office is
not a Whitby job, and the postal code the visitor types is the only location
this business acts on. A test asserts no location prop reaches the form.

It does not move the form above the local content. A page whose entire job
is to establish that this business works in this place spends that
credibility if it leads with the ask. The order is: why you should believe
we work here, then the ask, then the NAP block — which stays last and stays
interpolated from BUSINESS_NAP, because a local page stating the address
differently from the homepage is the most common reason a local entity fails
to resolve.

KNOWN AND DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS PATCH

Fourteen other templates still link to /#quote without a form: the footer,
404, the portal pages, /team, /about, /technical-library, /framework,
/resources, /service-areas, /where-we-work, /guides/[slug], /products/
floorforge. Most are low-intent or navigational and the homepage anchor is
the right target for them. /service-areas, /where-we-work and /guides/[slug]
are arguable and are a separate decision, not something to fold silently into
a patch about the detail pages.

Verified: 64 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
in this sandbox. verify:hygiene fails on a TRACKED
ECOWOODS_SALE_02_specification.patch that is sitting on origin/main
unapplied — that is HYG-02 doing its job on a real artifact, it predates this
patch, and it clears the moment SALE-02 is applied. parse-scan 614 files,
zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean on both templates, after fixing
the harness a third time: `notFound()` was stubbed as `any`, so it never
narrowed away `undefined` and reported 38 phantom TS18048s across code I had
not touched.

19 new assertions.

